    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d825">

                <pb facs="0720" n="708" sameAs="frus:frus1942v04/pg708"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.20 Defense (M)/5152: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Chargé in Turkey</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Kelley</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-02-03T14:00:00-05:00">February 3, 1942—2
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>34. …</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>3d. The Department is considering recommending preclusive purchasing of other
                    Turkish products.<anchor n="39" xml:id="d825-f1"/> Please telegraph your
                    recommendations <pb facs="0721" n="709" xml:id="pg709"/> as to the possibilities
                    of buying the Turkish products in which Germany has shown the greatest interest,
                    including hides, wool and possibly tobacco. The Department has had discussions
                    with Ambassador Steinhardt<anchor n="40" xml:id="d825-f2"/> on the subject, but
                    pending his arrival in Ankara the Department desires this information in order
                    to formulate its plans. In your reply please specify amounts of stocks and the
                    production for a 12-month period for each commodity the purchase of which you
                    recommend, and give your estimates of the prices which in your opinion would
                    have to be offered to obtain contracts for such stocks and such future
                    production.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d825-f1" n="39" xml:id="d825-f1-content">
                <p>Other than antimony discussed in paragraph 1 and copper discussed in paragraph 2.
                    For a discussion of the preclusive buying program in Turkey at this time, as
                    formulated by the British Government, see W. N. Medlicott, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">The Economic Blockade</hi> (London, 1959), vol. <hi
                        rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>, pp. 242 ff., in the series <hi
                        rendition="#italic">History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Civil
                        Series</hi>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d825-f2" n="40" xml:id="d825-f2-content">
                <p>Laurence A. Steinhardt, appointed Ambassador to Turkey; previously Ambassador in
                    the Soviet Union.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
